<!--
    Screen-reader names for control-template parts that ship WITHOUT one.

    A control's default template can contribute interactive elements that the markup
    hosting the control never sees, so no amount of AutomationProperties.Name in a view
    reaches them, and a static AXAML scan cannot find them either — there is no element
    in the markup to scan.  Naming them is the theme's job, which is why this file sits
    beside the theme bundle rather than in any one app.

    The /template/ selector is required throughout: without it a style does not reach an
    element that lives inside a control template.  Which control owns a part is a fact
    about the template, not a guess — `PART_TextBox` below is a child of the
    NumericUpDown's own template even though it renders inside the ButtonSpinner, so
    scoping it to ButtonSpinner would match nothing.  TemplatePartAutomationNameTests reads
    TemplatedParent to keep both facts honest.

    ⚠ The ELEMENT TYPE in a selector is a fact about the template too, and UIA cannot supply it:
    a UIA walk reports a control's PEER type, so the TreeViewItem chevron below reads as "Button"
    while the element is a ToggleButton.  A selector written from the walk matches nothing, builds
    with zero errors and zero warnings, and silently leaves the part unnamed.

    Guards: LayeredEditors.Avalonia.Tests/Themes/TemplatePartAutomationNameTests.cs and
    TreeChevronAutomationNameTests.cs apply this file's styles to real templated controls and read
    their automation peers.
-->
<Styles xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        xmlns:loc="using:Bennewitz.Ninja.LayeredEditors.Avalonia.Localization">

    <!--
        ── Fixed names: the NumericUpDown spinner buttons ────────────────────────────

        NumericUpDown draws a ButtonSpinner, whose template puts two RepeatButtons beside
        the text box.  Their Content is a PathIcon, and Avalonia's
        ContentControlAutomationPeer falls back to Content.ToString() when no name is set
        — so a screen reader announced each one as the literal string
        "Avalonia.Controls.PathIcon".  Measured through UIA 2026-09-07: twelve such
        buttons across ClaudeForge, six on OpenCodeForge's Essentials page alone.

        These two names are constants, so they come from WrapperStrings and a host can
        localise them through the same Resolver hook the wrapper chrome uses.
    -->
    <Style Selector="ButtonSpinner /template/ RepeatButton#PART_IncreaseButton">
        <Setter Property="AutomationProperties.Name"
                Value="{x:Static loc:WrapperStrings.LabelSpinnerIncrease}" />
    </Style>

    <Style Selector="ButtonSpinner /template/ RepeatButton#PART_DecreaseButton">
        <Setter Property="AutomationProperties.Name"
                Value="{x:Static loc:WrapperStrings.LabelSpinnerDecrease}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        ── TreeViewItem expand/collapse chevron ─────────────────────────────────────────

        The navigation tree's chevrons reached a screen reader as an unnamed "button" — 26 of
        them on one pass of scripts/Audit-Accessibility.ps1 (finding F6).  Like the spinner
        buttons above, the element is a template part no view declares, so nothing in markup
        could name it and the AXAML scan had no element to flag.

        ⛔ NAMED rather than hidden, and the reasoning that says otherwise is WRONG HERE.  The
        tempting fix is AutomationProperties.AccessibilityView="Raw" on the grounds that the
        TreeViewItem already exposes an ExpandCollapse pattern, making the chevron a duplicate.
        Measured on a real templated TreeViewItem instead of assumed:

            ITEM     peer=TreeViewItemAutomationPeer   interfaces: IScrollProvider,
                                                                   ISelectionItemProvider
            CHEVRON  peer=ToggleButtonAutomationPeer   interfaces: IToggleProvider

        There is no IExpandCollapseProvider on the item.  The chevron's IToggleProvider is the
        ONLY programmatic expand/collapse affordance the tree exposes, so hiding it would remove
        the affordance rather than de-duplicate it.

        ⚠ The selector says ToggleButton, not Button.  A UIA walk reports this element's CONTROL
        TYPE as Button — that is the peer's answer, not the element's — and a selector written
        from that reading matches nothing and fails silently, which is the same shape of trap as
        scoping a part to the wrong templated parent.  Verified: TemplatedParent is TreeViewItem.

        ⓘ The chevron is Focusable=False, so naming it does not add a tab stop.
    -->
    <Style Selector="TreeViewItem /template/ ToggleButton#PART_ExpandCollapseChevron">
        <Setter Property="AutomationProperties.Name"
                Value="{x:Static loc:WrapperStrings.LabelExpandCollapse}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        ── Inherited names: the element that actually takes focus ────────────────────

        NumericUpDown and AutoCompleteBox are composite: each is correctly named by its
        view and surfaces under its own control type (Spinner, and Group for
        AutoCompleteBox), but neither ever HOLDS focus.  Focus goes to the inner
        PART_TextBox, and that TextBox had no name and no LabeledBy — measured through
        UIA 2026-09-07 with HasKeyboardFocus, which read True on the inner Edit and False
        on the named Spinner above it.  So the AutomationProperties.Name every view
        carefully sets sat on an element a screen-reader user never lands on: 6
        NumericUpDowns and 8 AutoCompleteBoxes across ClaudeForge.

        There is no string to localise here — the name is the host control's own name,
        which the view already supplies in the user's language.  Copying it down means a
        screen reader may hear it twice, once as container context and once for the
        focused field; that is a verbosity cost, and it is the right side of the trade,
        because the alternative is a focused edit field with no name at all.

        A control whose name the view leaves unset copies down an empty string, which
        changes nothing — this cannot mask a missing name, it only relocates a present
        one.
    -->
    <Style Selector="NumericUpDown /template/ TextBox#PART_TextBox">
        <Setter Property="AutomationProperties.Name"
                Value="{Binding $parent[NumericUpDown].(AutomationProperties.Name)}" />
    </Style>

    <Style Selector="AutoCompleteBox /template/ TextBox#PART_TextBox">
        <Setter Property="AutomationProperties.Name"
                Value="{Binding $parent[AutoCompleteBox].(AutomationProperties.Name)}" />
    </Style>

</Styles>
